PART 2: UNDERSTANDING THE MAP FUNCTION

================================================================================

What is map()?

map()
is:

• A JavaScript array method • Transforms each item in an array • Returns a new array with transformed items • Does NOT modify the original array • Perfect for rendering lists in React

map() Syntax:

array.map((item, index) => {
  // Do something with item
  return transformed_item
})
Parameters:

Parameter	Description
item
Current item in the array
index
Position of item (0, 1, 2, ...)
array
The entire array (rarely used)
================================================================================

Basic map() Example (Non-React)

// Regular JavaScript - not React
const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

// Double each number
const doubled = numbers.map(num => num * 2)

console.log(doubled)  // [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]
What this does:

numbers.map(num => num * 2) → Loops through each number → Multiplies by 2 → Returns new array with doubled values

================================================================================

map() with Objects

// Regular JavaScript - not React
const books = [
  { id: 1, title: 'Book 1' },
  { id: 2, title: 'Book 2' },
  { id: 3, title: 'Book 3' }
]

// Extract just titles
const titles = books.map(book => book.title)

console.log(titles)  // ['Book 1', 'Book 2', 'Book 3']
What this does:

books.map(book => book.title) → Loops through each book → Extracts the title property → Returns array of titles

