PART 3: USING MAP() IN REACT

================================================================================

Basic map() in JSX

export default function BookList() {
  const books = [
    { id: 1, title: 'The Great Gatsby' },
    { id: 2, title: '1984' },
    { id: 3, title: 'To Kill a Mockingbird' }
  ]

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Books</h1>
      <ul>
        {books.map(book => (
          <li key={book.id}>{book.title}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  )
}
What this code does:

{books.map(book => (...))} → Loops through books array → For each book, creates JSX

<li key={book.id}>{book.title}</li> → Creates list item for each book → Displays book title
key={book.id} → Unique identifier for each item → Helps React track changes

Output:

Books
- The Great Gatsby
- 1984
- To Kill a Mockingbird
